{
 "description": "How a client reads /remind (docs/FEATURES.md sections 9.2 and 9.3): /remind [target] when | what, args being what follows the name. zone is the account's time zone (its time_zone, or the server's when it has none), which the client passes to the parser; now is the moment it is read, as wall-clock time in zone (a Tuesday). A case may give its own now and zone. Times in want are wall clock in zone, except one ending in Z, which is that exact moment. Wall clock is read in zone, never the device's: a time the zone's clocks skip moves on by the length of the gap (02:30 becomes 03:30), one they show twice is the first; a duration is exact. A typed instant (MESSAGE-FORMAT.md 3.1) as the when, alone or after at or on, with its own zone or offset is that exact moment, and without one is wall clock in zone; one its own zone skips is bad_when. want gives target (kind me, the default; person with the username, and its server for someone on another; or channel with the id of a channel link), what (everything after the first |, as typed), and either at, the one moment it runs (sent as run_at in RFC 3339 UTC), or repeat, the rule of 9.3 with zone as its time_zone (the first run is the server's to work out). error is the code a client refuses with before sending anything, one of errors, worded by the client. With nothing at all after /remind a client opens its reminder form instead. Both clients are held to these (e2e/commands.mjs, app/test/core/remind_test.dart); internal/sched checks that the server takes every repeat.",
